If the sun exploded right now, how long would it take for people on Earth to notice if time on Earth suddenly froze the moment it happened?	1. How long does it take light from the Sun to reach Earth?	This question contains a logical paradox. If time on Earth froze the moment the Sun exploded, people on Earth would never notice it because frozen time means no perception or consciousness is possible.	High	This is a paradoxical question. Light from the Sun takes about 8 minutes and 20 seconds to reach Earth. However, if time on Earth froze at the exact moment of the explosion, then by definition, no perceptual processes could occur on Earth. Frozen time means frozen observers who cannot register any new information, including the light from the explosion that would arrive 8 minutes and 20 seconds later.	1. NASA - 'Sun-Earth Connection' - Distance from Sun to Earth is 1 AU (about 93 million miles or 150 million km)	STEM	Physics/Philosophy	Both Google AI Search Mode and Gemini Flash 2.0 failed by attempting to calculate the time it would take light to reach Earth (~8 minutes) without addressing the paradox of frozen time, which would make perception impossible.

